<section class="section section--dark">
  <div class="wrap-hero" style="text-align:left">
    <h1>Welcome to the Stroud Lab!</h1>
    <p>
      The Stroud Lab is led by <a href="/james-stroud/">James Stroud</a>, an
      evolutionary ecologist at Georgia Tech who studies how lizards evolve in the wild.
    </p>
    <p class="lead">
      Why are there so many species, and why do they live where they live?
     </p>
         <p>
      We are an evolutionary ecology lab built on a single premise: ecology and
      evolution are not separable. Our central ambition is to bridge the
      process-pattern divide, connecting ecological and evolutionary dynamics
      measured in wild populations to the large-scale patterns of diversity that
      emerge across continents and clades.
    </p>
    <p>
      Most of this work happens in the field. We combine targeted sampling of
      populations with long-term mark-recapture studies that measure natural
      selection directly, letting us ask how selection shapes the phenotypes we
      observe. Nearly all of our work uses lizards, mostly <em>Anolis</em> lizards
      (anoles), at field sites across the southeastern United States (especially
      Florida), the Caribbean, and Central and South America.
    </p>
    <p>
      To do this, we integrate behavioral, computational, genomic, biomechanical,
      and physiological approaches, pairing field data with theoretical models and
      large-scale comparative datasets. Back at Georgia Tech, we are developing
      machine-learning tools for high-throughput phenotyping and for quantifying
      the structural environment, and building our own wearable sensors to record
      behavior passively in the wild. Welcome to the world of lizard backpacks!
    </p>
    <p>
      Solving the biodiversity crisis will require understanding not just which
      species exist, but why they live where they do and how rapidly they can
      respond to a changing world.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can read more about our research on the <a href="/publications/">Publications page</a>!
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

